📝 Notebook note

Write:

Creating a 3D object in Three.js
Geometry
   +
Material
   ↓
Mesh
   ↓
scene.add(mesh)

Example:

const geometry = new THREE.BoxGeometry(4, 0.5, 1);

const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
    color: 0x8B4513
});

const object = new THREE.Mesh(
    geometry,
    material
);

scene.add(object);
One important change from our cube

Previously we had:

cube.rotation.x += 0.01;

We removed that.

Why?

Because the support should stay still.

Later, we'll create a separate pivot object that rotates. The rod and bob will become children of that pivot.

That will demonstrate the transformation hierarchy we studied.

Support
   │
   └── Pivot
        │
        └── Rod
             │
             └── Bob

That's where our pendulum starts becoming an actual graphics project rather than just a collection of shapes.

Checkpoint

Run the code and make sure you can see the brown support bar.

Once you see it, we'll add the pivot + rod next.

📝 Notebook

Write this:

Transformation hierarchy

A parent object can contain child objects. Transformations applied to the parent can affect its children.

Example:

const parent = new THREE.Group();

parent.add(child);

For our pendulum:

Pivot
 └── Rod
      └── Bob

Next: we'll add the pendulum bob (weight) at the bottom. Then we'll finally make the whole thing swing.